 of Linear Equations Using Elimination: One Transformation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050190 #edb/ref 1033 4026 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050190 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "35070aa4-19de-5bfc-b9cc-08a2e69ce882" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050452 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048511 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050452 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050452 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049538 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050452 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049636 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050452 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049782 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050452 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050575 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050452 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054788 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050452 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050452 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054790 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050452 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054791 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050452 #edb/ref 1031 "Collecting Like Terms in Linear Expressions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050452 #edb/ref 1033 134 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050452 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "4a33a1d4-8dfa-5e25-8ed4-4aa1496ac8a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050970 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050552 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050970 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186217204 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050970 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186217205 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050970 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186217206 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050970 #edb/ref 1031 "Constructing Linear Functions Using Slope-Intercept Form" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050970 #edb/ref 1033 6253 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050970 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "766c401c-744a-5777-b581-dc67414866be" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082932 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13993" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082932 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the solution $(a,b)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n\n${a + b = 3; 4a - b = 7$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082932 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "06136eef-284e-548f-a57b-f400a05f2cb9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082932 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the coefficient of $b$ in the first equation is $1$, and the coefficient of $b$ in the second equation is $-1$.\n\nSince these numbers add to zero, we can add the two equations, *eliminate* the variable $b$, and solve the resulting equation for $a$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na + b &= 3 \\\\\n4a - b &= 7 \\\\\n(a + 4a) &= (3 + 7) \\\\\n5a &= 10 \\\\\na &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $a=2$. However, we still need to find $b$. To do this, we substitute $a=2$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na + b &= 3 \\\\\n(2) + b &= 3 \\\\\nb &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $a=2, \\,b=1$, which can be expressed as $(2, 1)$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082933 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13994" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082933 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the solution $(a,b)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n${3a - b = 2; - 3a + 2b =-1$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082933 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "17cb905e-e692-59b8-964d-c7b50482c11e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082933 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the coefficient of $a$ in the first equation is $3$, and the coefficient of $a$ in the second equation is $-3$.\n\nSince these numbers add to zero, we can add the two equations, *eliminate* the variable $a$, and solve the resulting equation for $b$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3a - b &= 2 \\\\\n- 3a + 2b &= -1 \\\\\n(-b + 2b) &= (2 + (-1)) \\\\\nb &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $b=1$. However, we still need to find $a$. To do this, we substitute $b=1$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3a - b &= 2 \\\\\n3a - (1) &= 2 \\\\\n3a &= 3 \\\\\na &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $a=1, \\,b=1$, which can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\left(1,1\\right)\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082934 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13996" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082934 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n${x + y = 3; 6x - 2y =-2$\n\nThe variable $y$ can be eliminated from the system using the following two steps:\n\n1. Multiply the first equation by $2$ and obtain\n$2x + 2y|= 6$.\n2. Add the multiplied equation to the second equation to get\n$⋯?⋯ = 4$.\n\nWhat's the missing expression?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082934 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "98645504-986a-5847-8042-32ced1cf4d14" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082934 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's follow the steps provided:\n\n**Step 1**: Multiplying the first equation by ${\\color{blue}{2}}$ yields the following equivalent system:\n\n$$\n{(x + y = 3) \\cdot (2); 6x - 2y =-2\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we add these two equations together:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} \\begin{array}{rl} 2x + \\cancel{2y} & = 6 \\\\[5pt] 6x - \\cancel{2y} & = -2 \\\\[5pt] \\hline (2x + 6x) &= (6 - 2) \\\\[5pt] {\\color{red}8x} &= 4 \\end{array}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is ${\\color{red}8x}$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082935 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14000" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082935 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following system of equations.\n${3x - 2y = 9; x - 3y =-4$\n\nThe variable $x$ can be eliminated from the system using the following two steps:\n\n1. Multiply the second equation by $-3$ and obtain\n$-3x + 9y = 12$.\n2. Add the multiplied equation to the first equation to get\n$⋯?⋯ = 21$.\n\nFind the missing expression, and hence find the solution $(x,y)$ of the system." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082935 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0e96914f-2f28-5fef-8a36-9cf2869b1b43" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082935 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's follow the steps provided:\n\n**Step 1**: Multiplying the second equation by ${\\color{blue}{-3}}$ yields the following equivalent system:\n\n$$\n{(3x - 2y = 9; (x - 3y =-4) \\cdot (-3)\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we add these two equations together, *eliminate* the variable $x$, and solve the resulting equation for $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} \\begin{array}{rl} \\cancel{3x} - 2y & = 9 \\\\[5pt] -\\cancel{3x} + 9y & = 12 \\\\[5pt] \\hline (9y - 2y) & = (9 + 12) \\\\[5pt] {\\color{red}7y} & = 21 \\\\[5pt] y & = 3 \\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $y = 3$. However, we still need to find $x$. To do this, we substitute $y = 3$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - 3y &= -4 \\\\\nx - 3(3) &= -4 \\\\\nx - 9 &= -4 \\\\\nx &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is\n\n$$\nx=5, \\,y = 3\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed as $(5,3)$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083168 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048673 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083168 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048678 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083168 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083168 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186083605 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083168 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186083606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083168 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186083927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083168 #edb/ref 1039 "Eliminating the Second Variable" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083168 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186082932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083168 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "7e0dbf68-2247-5a47-aac8-c16acc5e710c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083169 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186083485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083169 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186083607 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083169 #edb/ref 1039 "Eliminating the First Variable" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083169 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186082933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083169 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "e562941a-078d-555f-aab6-c59d4c79dfba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083170 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186083608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083170 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186083609 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083170 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186083779 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083170 #edb/ref 1039 "Determining the Result of a Transformation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083170 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186082934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083170 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "bc55e7f0-598a-5244-84ef-6dff74f0c420" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083171 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186083568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083171 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186083780 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083171 #edb/ref 1039 "Solving a System of Equations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083171 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186082935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083171 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "20e61a36-c5bf-598a-a07d-833611ffaee6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083433 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186084021 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083433 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186084022 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083433 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186084023 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083433 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186084024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083433 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186084025 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083433 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186084026 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083433 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083433 #edb/ref 1045 "Introduction to the Elimination Method" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083433 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186084021 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083433 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049060 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083433 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "a68b3f7f-449b-5dcc-876b-2a9dda0b7af9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084021 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084022 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084021 #edb/ref 1061 143346 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084021 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "22b878ba-aa9a-5218-889c-006c8dcacca7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084021 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186084514 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084022 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084023 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084022 #edb/ref 1061 143347 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084022 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5234accd-01b6-501e-866d-11b931397534" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084022 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083168 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084023 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084023 #edb/ref 1061 143351 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084023 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "adba60f0-2f20-5985-8599-c86e9c30f9db" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084023 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084024 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084025 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084024 #edb/ref 1061 143354 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084024 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1c45a64c-2352-5510-9c77-6d975e32ee8a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084024 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186084515 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084025 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084026 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084025 #edb/ref 1061 143355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084025 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f6f3680b-161b-5ba4-b393-bb9b5b36236b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084025 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083170 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084026 #edb/ref 1061 143359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084026 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b6308dc5-5a40-5abe-9f8e-91524b3b2359" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084026 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083171 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084514 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that we want to solve, *without using graphs*, the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{x + y = 1; 5x - y = 11\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we must combine the equations into a single equation that can be solved using algebraic manipulation. One way to combine the equations is to use the **elimination method.**\n\nNotice that the coefficient of $y$ in the first equation is $1$, and the coefficient of $y$ in the second equation is $-1$. Since these numbers add to zero, we can *add* the two equations, *eliminate* the variable $y$, and solve the resulting equation for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + y &= 1 \\\\\n5x - y &= 11 \\\\\n(x + 5x) &= (1 + 11) \\\\\n6x &= 12 \\\\\nx &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x=2$. However, we still need to find $y$. To do this, we substitute $x=2$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + y &= 1 \\\\\n(2) + y &= 1 \\\\\ny &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=2$ and $y=-1$, which can be expressed as $(2, -1)$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084514 #edb/ref 1065 8702 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084514 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "48f030ba-6728-565a-be53-cfb1e88abc86" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084514 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084515 #edb/ref 1064 "Now, suppose that we want to solve the following system of linear equations using elimination:\n\n$$\n{x + 2y = 1; x - y =-2\n$$\n\nAs it stands, adding the two equations will not eliminate any of the variables.\n\nHowever, suppose we want to eliminate the variable $x$. We begin by multiplying the second equation by $\\color{blue}-1$, which gives the following equivalent system:\n\n$$\n{(x + 2y = 1; (x - y =-2)\\cdot (-1)\n$$\n\nThen, we add the two resulting equations together. This eliminates the variable $x$, making it possible to solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + 2y &= 1 \\\\\n- x + y &= 2 \\\\\n(2y + y) &= (1 + 2) \\\\\n3y &= 3 \\\\\ny &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $y=1$. However, we still need to find $x$. To do this, we substitute $y=1$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - y &= -2 \\\\\nx - (1) &= -2 \\\\\nx &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-1$ and $y=1$, which can be expressed as $(-1, 1)$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084515 #edb/ref 1065 8704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084515 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "170be181-ae77-50d7-a5f1-0a4bdcfa318e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084515 #edb/ref 1067 "Eliminating Variables From Systems of Linear Equations" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
